      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "STUDY OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To describe a technique of uterine suspension using round ligaments to relieve pain in selected patients with various degrees of uterine retroversion."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Prospective clinical study (Canadian Task Force classification II-2)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "University-based center for reproductive medicine."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "Thirty women who underwent laparoscopy for investigation of chronic pelvic pain (CPP) and dyspareunia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTION",
          "content": "Round ligaments were plicated using a modification of intracorporeal knot tying during laparoscopy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS",
          "content": "The extent of plication was planned to elevate the uterus and bring it minimally forward. Mean +/- SD operating time was 14+/-4 minutes. Pain scores before and after surgery were 4.5+/-1.0 and 1.6+/-0.6, respectively (p<0.001). There were no complications during or after surgery. Only three women had minimal dyspareunia postoperatively, although they had anteverted uteri. One patient had severe dyspareunia that developed 7 months after surgery and continued through the second year of follow-up. Nineteen women with anteverted uteri were free of dyspareunia after 2 years."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Round ligament plication is safe and effective in patients with retroverted uteri and dyspareunia or CPP."
        }
      ]},
